{%- comment -%}
  FAQ — per gem
{%- endcomment -%}
  {%- case gem_handle -%}
    {%- when 'emerald' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--emerald">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><em>Are Emeralds naturally included? Yes. Natural Emeralds typically contain inclusions known as </em>jardin — their internal “garden.” These are expected, and in a well-balanced stone, they add character rather than reduce beauty. A completely clean Emerald is unusual and should always be examined carefully.</li>
          <li><strong>Are Emeralds treated?</strong> Most natural Emeralds receive light to moderate colourless oiling, which is a traditional and widely accepted treatment. This should always be disclosed. We do not consider heavy resin, polymer, wax, acceptable for premium or astrological use.</li>
          <li><strong>Can Panna be worn daily?</strong> Yes — with mindful care and a secure setting. Emerald is suitable for regular wear, but it should be treated more gently than harder stones. Think of it as a gem with character, not one that responds well to force.</li>
          <li><strong>Which metal pairs best with Emerald?</strong> Gold is traditionally preferred for Emerald in Vedic astrology. Silver may be used only when specifically advised based on the horoscope.</li>
          <li><strong>Does Emerald change colour over time?</strong> No. Natural Emeralds do not “lose” their colour. If a stone appears to change, it is usually because stabilising oils have been affected by heat, chemicals, or harsh cleaning — not because the gem itself is unstable.</li>
          <li><strong>What is the ideal weight for astrological use?</strong> Typically 2–7 carats, depending on the chart and the strength of Mercury. As always, quality, colour presence, and suitability matter more than size alone.</li>
          <li><strong>Is Emerald suitable for astrological use?</strong> Yes — when it is appropriate for the wearer’s horoscope and worn specifically to support Mercury (Budh). Suitability should always be confirmed before wearing.</li>
          <li><strong>How do I verify a genuine Emerald?</strong> Ask for a third-party lab certificate that confirms: natural Emerald, treatment type disclosed, no resin or polymer filling, and online verification available. If a seller is vague about treatment or origin, that is a red flag.</li>
          <li><strong>Is a flawless Emerald always better?</strong> No. Emeralds are expected to have inclusions. A stone with lively colour, good structural balance, and honest natural character is often far more valuable than one that appears unnaturally perfect.</li>
          <li><strong>How is Emerald different from other Mercury stones?</strong> Emerald (Panna) is the primary gemstone of Mercury and the strongest Budh ratna. It works more directly and more distinctly than substitute stones, which tend to act more gently. This is why proper consultation before wearing is essential.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    {%- when 'blue-sapphire' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--blue_sapphire">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions — Neelam / Blue Sapphire</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Does Blue Sapphire change colour over time?</strong> No. Natural and properly heat-treated Blue Sapphires are colour-stable for life. A sudden visible change almost always points to unstable treatment — not the stone itself.</li>
          <li><strong>Are Blue Sapphires treated?</strong> Most are either untreated or heat-treated. Both are acceptable when disclosed. We do not carry glass-filled or diffusion-treated stones under any circumstances.</li>
          <li><strong>Can Neelam be worn daily?</strong> Yes. At Mohs 9, Blue Sapphire is exceptionally durable and well-suited to daily wear when securely set.</li>
          <li><strong>Which metal pairs best with Blue Sapphire?</strong> Silver or Panchdhatu traditionally. White Gold may be used when astrologically advised.</li>
          <li><strong>How soon do the effects of Blue Sapphire appear?</strong> Typically within 3–8 weeks, provided the stone is suited to the wearer's horoscope. Effects are gradual and cumulative — not sudden.</li>
          <li><strong>What is the ideal weight for astrological use?</strong> 2–7 carats, guided by the horoscope and the strength of Saturn's placement.</li>
          <li><strong>Can Blue Sapphire be worn during Sadhe Saati?</strong> Yes — but only when Saturn is favourable in the chart and after proper astrological testing or guidance. Do not wear it on assumption alone. Sadhe Saati affects everyone differently, and the stone must match the individual, not the transit.</li>
          <li><strong>Is a flawless Blue Sapphire always better?</strong> No. Natural Blue Sapphires typically contain inclusions. A stone with balanced colour, fine natural silk, and structural stability is far more valuable than one that looks unnaturally perfect. Perfection in a sapphire is often a reason to look closer, not pay more.</li>
          <li><strong>How is Neelam different from other Saturn stones?</strong> Blue Sapphire (Neelam) is the primary gemstone of Saturn and the strongest among Shani ratnas. It works directly on Saturn's energy in the chart — unlike substitute stones, which work more gently. This is why expert consultation before wearing is essential.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    {%- when 'opal' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--opal">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Do Opals change colour or fade?</strong> Natural Opals have stable play-of-colour. Australian Opals rarely fade under normal conditions. Ethiopian Opals may turn temporarily cloudy with water exposure due to their hydrophane nature — this is usually temporary, not permanent damage.</li>
          <li><strong>Are your Opals treated or coated?</strong> No. Every Opal we carry is 100% natural, untreated, and uncoated.</li>
          <li><strong>Which is better — Australian or Ethiopian?</strong> For astrological wear: Australian Opal. For jewellery at a more accessible budget: Ethiopian Opal, with the understanding that it requires extra care around moisture.</li>
          <li><strong>Can Opal be worn for Venus-related issues?</strong> Yes. Opal is one of the most commonly recommended gemstones for Shukra (Venus), particularly when Diamond or White Sapphire are not suited to the wearer's chart.</li>
          <li><strong>What is the ideal weight for astrological use?</strong> Traditionally 6–7 ratti (approximately 5–6 carats). Quality, glow, and structural stability matter more than hitting an exact number.</li>
          <li><strong>How soon do benefits appear?</strong> Typically within 3–6 weeks, depending on the wearer's chart and the strength of Venus in the horoscope. Effects are gradual, not dramatic.</li>
          <li><strong>Why do Ethiopian Opals absorb water?</strong> They are hydrophane — a natural property of their formation that makes them porous and moisture-sensitive. It is not a defect, but it does require more careful handling.</li>
          <li><strong>Are doublets and triplets real Opals?</strong> They may contain a thin layer of natural Opal, but they are composite stones, not solid Opals. They are not suitable for astrological use and should never be sold as full natural Opals.</li>
          <li><strong>Is a flawless Opal always better?</strong> No. Look for lively play-of-colour that shifts with movement, multiple visible colour flashes, a smooth surface, and stable structure. Unusual perfection or suspiciously low pricing are reasons to look closer, not pay more.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    {%- when 'ruby' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--ruby">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Is Ruby suitable for astrological use? Yes — when it suits the horoscope and is worn for </strong>Surya (Sun) with proper guidance and confirmation of suitability.</li>
          <li><strong>Are your Rubies treated?</strong> We carry only natural Rubies that are either untreated or heat-treated, with full disclosure on every certification. We do not carry glass-filled, lead-filled, or diffusion-treated stones under any circumstances.</li>
          <li><strong>What is the best Ruby origin?</strong> Burma remains the classic benchmark for colour and prestige. Mozambique is highly respected and increasingly sought after. Other origins vary in character and quality — what matters most is that origin is disclosed and independently verified.</li>
          <li><strong>What is the correct weight?</strong> Traditionally 2–7 carats, guided by the horoscope and the strength of the Sun’s placement. Colour strength and stability matter more than reaching a specific weight.</li>
          <li><strong>How soon do benefits appear?</strong> Typically within 4–6 weeks, depending on the horoscope and how well the stone suits the wearer’s Sun placement.</li>
          <li><strong>Can Ruby be worn daily? Yes. At </strong>Mohs 9, Ruby is highly durable for daily wear when securely set.</li>
          <li><strong>Which metal pairs best?</strong> Gold, traditionally. Panchdhatu or Copper-alloy when advised astrologically.</li>
          <li><strong>Does Ruby lose colour over time?</strong> No. Natural Ruby retains its colour permanently. Fading is almost always a sign of glass filling or poor treatment — not the stone itself.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    {%- when 'yellow-sapphire' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--yellow_sapphire">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Why do some Yellow Sapphires lose colour?</strong> Usually due to unstable natural colour centres or irradiation treatment that fades over time. Always ask specifically for colour-stability assurance — it is non-negotiable for astrological use.</li>
          <li><strong>Are your Yellow Sapphires treated?</strong> We carry only natural Yellow Sapphires that are either untreated or heat-treated, with full disclosure on every certification. No diffusion-treated or glass-filled stones, ever.</li>
          <li><strong>Which origin is best for astrology?</strong> Untreated stones from Sri Lanka, Burma, and Madagascar are generally the most trusted for astrological purposes. Sri Lanka remains the classic benchmark.</li>
          <li><strong>What is the correct weight?</strong> Traditionally 2–7 carats (approximately 2.25–8 ratti). Quality, clarity, and colour stability matter more than hitting a specific weight.</li>
          <li><strong>How soon do benefits appear?</strong> Typically within 4–6 weeks, depending on Jupiter’s placement and the strength of its influence in the wearer’s chart.</li>
          <li><strong>Can it be worn daily? Yes. At </strong>Mohs 9, Yellow Sapphire is highly durable for daily wear when securely set.</li>
          <li><strong>Which metal pairs best?</strong> Gold, traditionally. Panchdhatu may also be used when advised.</li>
          <li><strong>Can Yellow Sapphire be worn with other gemstones?</strong> Yes — but compatibility depends entirely on the individual horoscope and should always be confirmed through proper astrological guidance before combining stones.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    {%- when 'white-sapphire' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--white_sapphire">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <p>Is White Sapphire a Diamond substitute? No. It is a distinct gemstone valued for its calm brilliance and Venusian properties, not as a replacement for Diamond.</p>
        <p>Are your White Sapphires treated? Only natural stones — untreated or heat-treated with full disclosure. No coating or glass filling.</p>
        <p>Which origin is best? Sri Lanka, Burma, and Madagascar all produce suitable stones. Quality of light and clarity matter more than origin alone.</p>
        <p>What is the correct weight? Typically 2–7 carats. Quality and balance matter more than size.</p>
        <p>How soon do benefits appear? Usually within 3–6 weeks, depending on Venus’s placement in the chart.</p>
        <p>Can it be worn daily? Yes. Its durability makes it ideal for everyday wear when properly set.</p>
        <p>Which metal is best? Gold or Silver — white metals are often preferred for their visual harmony.</p>
        <p>Does White Sapphire lose clarity? No. Natural stones remain stable. Any dullness is usually surface residue and can be cleaned easily.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'red-coral' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--red_coral">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <p><strong>Is Red Coral a gemstone or an organic material?</strong> It is an organic gemstone, formed from marine life rather than mineral deposits — which is what gives it its distinct character, care requirements, and astrological significance.</p>
        <p><strong>Does Red Coral wear out with use?</strong> Mild surface wear is natural over time and is not a cause for concern. It does not reduce astrological effectiveness. Professional repolishing can restore surface finish when needed.</p>
        <p><strong>Is fake Coral common in the market?</strong> Yes — more so than almost any other gemstone. Dyed, plastic, ceramic, resin, and reconstructed coral are widely sold. Always insist on independent certification before purchasing.</p>
        <p><strong>Which Coral is best for astrology?</strong> Italian (Sardinian) Red Coral is the traditional benchmark. Japanese (Aka) Red Coral is the refined premium choice. Both are natural, untreated, and well-suited for Mangal remedies.</p>
        <p><strong>Are ceramic or plastic Corals suitable for astrology?</strong> No. They carry no astrological value, wear quickly, and are a straightforward misrepresentation of what natural coral is.</p>
        <p><strong>Can Red Coral be worn daily?</strong> Yes, with mindful care. Rotating wear helps preserve the surface polish, and removing it before physical activity extends its longevity significantly.</p>
        <p><strong>Which metal is recommended?</strong> Gold or Copper traditionally. Silver only when astrologically advised.</p>
        <p><strong>Does surface wear reduce astrological effect?</strong> No. Minor physical wear over time does not diminish the astrological role of natural coral — only the quality and authenticity of the stone itself determines that.</p>
        <p><strong>Why is some Red Coral very cheap?</strong> Almost always because it is imitation, dyed, plastic, resin-based, or reconstructed material being sold as natural coral. True Red Coral value lives in natural origin, density, and honest disclosure.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'pearl' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--pearl">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <p><strong>Is Pearl a gemstone or an organic material?</strong> Pearl is an organic gemstone — formed naturally inside a mollusk over time. That origin is precisely what gives it its character, its care requirements, and its astrological significance.</p>
        <p><strong>Does Pearl wear out with use?</strong> A gradual, subtle loss of lustre is natural over years of wear — this is the nature of an organic gem and is entirely expected. It does not reduce astrological effectiveness.</p>
        <p><strong>Does surface wear reduce Pearl's astrological effect?</strong> No. Minor physical wear over time does not diminish the Moon's influence through the stone, provided the Pearl is natural and intact.</p>
        <p><strong>Which Pearl is best for astrology?</strong> Natural Basra Pearl is the most preferred. High-quality, undyed South Sea Pearl is widely accepted. Freshwater Pearls require careful selection. Dyed or imitation pearls carry no astrological value.</p>
        <p><strong>Are cultured pearls acceptable?</strong> Yes — when they are high-quality, thick-nacre, and undyed. Cultured pearls are widely accepted for astrological use when natural pearls are unavailable or outside budget.</p>
        <p><strong>Can Pearl be worn daily?</strong> Yes, with mindful care. Rotating wear and wiping after each use will preserve the lustre significantly over time.</p>
        <p><strong>Which metal should Pearl be set in?</strong> Silver traditionally. Gold when astrologically advised.</p>
        <p><strong>How can I tell if a Pearl is genuine?</strong> Insist on an independent third-party lab certificate confirming natural or cultured Pearl, with no dye or coating, verifiable online. A genuine pearl will also feel slightly gritty if rubbed gently against the teeth — a smooth, glassy feel suggests imitation.</p>
        <p><strong>Why are some Pearls very cheap?</strong> Almost always plastic, glass, or shell imitations, or thin-nacre cultured pearls that will not hold their lustre. The difference becomes visible within months of wear.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'hessonite' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--hessonite">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <p><strong>What exactly is Hessonite?</strong> Hessonite is a variety of grossular garnet — a natural crystalline gemstone, not an organic material like Pearl or Coral — traditionally worn as a Vedic remedy for ☊ Rahu.</p>
        <p><strong>Is Hessonite always brown?</strong> No. Natural Hessonite ranges from honey and amber through cinnamon to cognac. Very dark or muddy stones are typically lower quality. The finest stones have a warm, earthy glow that feels alive rather than flat.</p>
        <p><strong>Is Hessonite usually treated?</strong> Most quality Hessonite is untreated. Light heat treatment may occur and must always be disclosed. We carry only stones with full treatment transparency.</p>
        <p><strong>Can it be worn daily?</strong> Yes. Hessonite is durable enough for regular daily wear when properly set and cared for.</p>
        <p><strong>Does it break easily?</strong> No — but like all gemstones, it can crack under strong impact or in a poorly made setting. A secure setting is important.</p>
        <p><strong>Which metal should it be set in?</strong> Silver is traditionally preferred. Gold may be used when astrologically advised.</p>
        <p><strong>Can Hessonite be worn with other gemstones?</strong> Yes — but combinations should always be guided by the individual chart. Do not combine gemstones without proper astrological assessment.</p>
        <p><strong>How soon do effects show?</strong> Typically within 30–60 days, depending on Rahu's placement and the specifics of the individual horoscope.</p>
        <p><strong>How do I verify a genuine Hessonite?</strong> Insist on an independent third-party lab certificate confirming natural Hessonite garnet, with treatment clearly disclosed and no glass filling, verifiable online.</p>
        <p><strong>Why is some Hessonite very cheap?</strong> Poor colour, dull appearance, synthetic or misrepresented material, or undisclosed treatments — almost always one or more of these. Quality Hessonite with genuine warmth and glow is not a commodity.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'cats-eye' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--cats_eye">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <p><strong>What exactly is Cat's Eye?</strong> A gemstone exhibiting chatoyancy — a moving band of reflected light caused by parallel needle-like inclusions within the stone. In Vedic astrology, Chrysoberyl Cat's Eye is the classical choice for ☋ Ketu.</p>
        <p><strong>Are all Cat's Eye stones astrologically effective?</strong> No. Only Chrysoberyl Cat's Eye is the true Lehsunia. Quartz Cat's Eye has limited, chart-dependent use. Fibre-optic, glass, and synthetic versions have no astrological value.</p>
        <p><strong>What colour should a good Cat's Eye be?</strong> Colour is secondary to eye quality. For Chrysoberyl, honey-green to yellowish tones are preferred. The eye's sharpness, continuity, and contrast matter far more than body colour alone.</p>
        <p><strong>Is Cat's Eye risky to wear?</strong> Yes — if worn without proper guidance. Ketu is a powerful and unpredictable influence. This stone should never be worn casually or on assumption. Always consult an experienced astrologer first.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'amethyst' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--amethyst">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <p>Is Amethyst a natural gemstone? Yes. It is a natural variety of quartz, formed in the earth and not synthetic or composite.</p>
        <p>Does Amethyst fade over time? High-quality natural Amethyst is colour-stable under ordinary wear. Prolonged exposure to heat or strong direct sunlight may gradually lighten the colour — normal daily wear and storage does not pose this risk.</p>
        <p>Is Amethyst usually treated? Most Amethyst is natural and untreated. Confirm this through independent certification before purchasing.</p>
        <p>Is darker always better? No. The best stones show balanced, even colour with good clarity — rich without being so dark that the stone loses transparency and life.</p>
        <p>Can it be worn daily? Yes. At Mohs 7, Amethyst is suitable for daily wear when properly set. It is softer than the corundum and chrysoberyl stones in this collection, so a protective setting is advisable for rings worn during physical activity.</p>
        <p>Is Amethyst used in healing or spiritual practice? Yes — it is widely associated with calm, focus, emotional balance, and meditative clarity, and has been used in contemplative traditions across cultures for centuries.</p>
        <p>Does origin matter? Origin affects tone and character — African tends toward deeper purple, Brazilian toward softer violet — but colour, clarity, and cut quality matter more than the origin label alone.</p>
        <p>Which metal pairs best? Silver and white metals are commonly preferred and complement the stone's violet tones naturally. Gold may also be used when preferred or astrologically advised.</p>
        <p>Why is some Amethyst very cheap? Pale or low-grade colour, poor cut, or imitation and misrepresented material. Quality Amethyst with even saturation and good clarity is not a commodity — the difference is visible and meaningful.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'citrine' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--citrine">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <p>Is Citrine a natural gemstone? Yes. Citrine is a natural variety of quartz, formed in the earth and distinct from synthetic or composite alternatives.</p>
        <p>Does Citrine fade or change colour? Natural Citrine is colour-stable under ordinary wear. Prolonged exposure to heat or strong direct sunlight may gradually lighten it — normal daily use does not present this risk.</p>
        <p>Is Citrine treated? Most Citrine is natural and untreated. Always ask for clear disclosure and confirm colour stability through independent certification.</p>
        <p>Is Citrine suitable for astrological use? Yes. Associated with ♃ Guru (Jupiter), it is widely chosen as a gentler, more accessible alternative to Yellow Sapphire for Jupiter-related remedies.</p>
        <p>What weight is recommended? There is no strict rule. Citrine is commonly worn between 2–15 carats depending on personal preference and astrological advice. Quality and colour presence matter more than reaching a specific weight.</p>
        <p>Can it be worn daily? Yes. At Mohs 7, Citrine is suitable for daily wear with proper care. A protective setting is advisable for rings worn during physical activity.</p>
        <p>Which metal pairs best? Silver, Gold, and Panchdhatu are all commonly used — the choice may depend on design preference or astrological guidance.</p>
        <p>Can Citrine be used for meditation or energy work? Yes. It is widely used in contemplative and energy practices for clarity, optimism, confidence, and mental balance — qualities that align naturally with Jupiter's influence.</p>
        <p>How do I verify a genuine Citrine? Request a natural Citrine confirmation with clear treatment disclosure from a credible independent lab. Any reputable seller should provide this readily.</p>
        <p>Why is some Citrine very cheap? Pale or low-grade colour, poor cut, or dyed quartz and imitation material. Quality Citrine with genuine warmth and transparency is not difficult to find — but it does require knowing what to look for and insisting on honest disclosure.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'turquoise' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--turquoise">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <p>What makes Turquoise different from other gemstones in this collection? Turquoise is a porous, organic-feeling stone with a sensitivity to moisture, oils, and chemicals that crystalline gems do not share. This makes it more care-intensive, but also gives it a warmth and character that polished stones often lack.</p>
        <p>Is stabilised Turquoise suitable for astrological use? High-quality stabilised Turquoise is generally accepted when disclosed. Natural, untreated stones are preferred where available and within budget.</p>
        <p>Can Turquoise change colour over time? Yes — if it absorbs sweat, oils, or chemicals it can darken permanently. Consistent care after every wear is the most effective prevention.</p>
        <p>Which origin is better — Iranian or American? Iranian Turquoise is the traditional benchmark for astrological use, valued for its fine texture and classic colour. American Turquoise offers striking character and is well regarded for jewellery. For astrological purposes, Iranian origin is generally preferred when accessible.</p>
        <p>Can Turquoise be worn daily? With care and a protective setting, yes — though rotating wear and removing it before any chemical contact significantly extends its life and preserves its colour.</p>
        <p>Which metal is best? Silver is most commonly used. Gold when preferred or astrologically advised. A bezel or semi-bezel setting is strongly recommended over prong settings for this stone.</p>
        <p>How do I verify a genuine Turquoise? Request an independent lab certificate confirming natural or stabilised Turquoise with treatment fully disclosed and origin stated. Any seller unable or unwilling to provide this should be treated with caution.</p>
        <p>Why is some Turquoise very cheap? Plastic, resin, ceramic, or reconstituted composite material — almost always. Genuine Turquoise with natural colour and honest disclosure is not a low-cost stone at meaningful quality levels.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'lapis-lazuli' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--lapis_lazuli">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <p>Is Lapis Lazuli a single mineral? No. It is a rock composed primarily of lazurite, typically with natural pyrite and calcite. This composition is precisely what gives it its distinctive appearance and character.</p>
        <p>Why does it have golden flecks? These are natural pyrite crystals. When fine and evenly dispersed, they are considered one of the stone's most beautiful and desirable features — the quality that has inspired the night sky comparison across so many cultures and centuries.</p>
        <p>Is Lapis commonly dyed? Yes. Commercial Lapis is frequently dyed to deepen colour or disguise lower-quality material. Always ask for clear treatment disclosure before purchasing.</p>
        <p>Can it be worn daily? Occasional, mindful wear is best. Lapis is softer and more porous than the crystalline stones in this collection, and benefits from protective settings and regular rest from wear.</p>
        <p>What is the best origin? Badakhshan, Afghanistan is the traditional and current benchmark for fine-quality Lapis Lazuli — a status it has held for thousands of years.</p>
        <p>How do I know if it is genuine? Look for dense, substantial feel, natural colour variation, no blue residue when wiped, and honest disclosure from a knowledgeable seller with independent certification to support their claims.</p>
        <p>Which metal suits it best? Silver is most commonly used and suits the stone's deep, contemplative character naturally. Gold may also be used depending on design preference.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'tourmaline' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--tourmaline">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <p>Is Tourmaline a natural gemstone? Yes. Tourmaline is a naturally occurring gemstone family known for its extraordinary colour range and natural crystal forms — one of the most diverse in the mineral world.</p>
        <p>Is it usually treated? Most Tourmaline is untreated. Gentle heat treatment is used in some cases and is acceptable when fully disclosed. Always confirm before purchasing.</p>
        <p>Which colours are most valuable? Fine Indicolite (blue), vivid green, and quality Rubellite (red/pink) command the highest prices. Bi-colour and Watermelon Tourmalines are prized more for their uniqueness and rarity of form than for colour intensity alone.</p>
        <p>Can it be worn daily? Yes. At Mohs 7–7.5, Tourmaline is suitable for regular daily wear when properly set. A protective setting is advisable for rings worn during physical activity.</p>
        <p>Is Tourmaline used for astrology? Tourmaline is not a primary Navratna gemstone. It is worn for balance, grounding, and emotional ease, and sometimes chosen as a gentler colour-based alternative to stronger planetary stones when those have been advised against or feel too intense.</p>
        <p>Which metal pairs best? Silver and white metals suit the cooler tones — blue, green, bi-colour. Gold complements the warmer stones — pink, red, and golden varieties. Final choice may depend on the specific colour of the stone and personal or astrological preference.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'aquamarine' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--aquamarine">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <p>Is Aquamarine a natural gemstone? Yes. It is a natural variety of beryl — the same mineral family as Emerald — distinguished by its blue to blue-green colour caused by iron.</p>
        <p>Is it usually treated? Gentle heat treatment is common, stable, and widely accepted. It removes unwanted greenish tones and produces a cleaner blue without affecting durability. Always confirm treatment status through independent certification.</p>
        <p>Can it be worn daily? Yes. Aquamarine is durable and suitable for regular daily wear when properly set and cared for.</p>
        <p>Which Aquamarine is most valuable? Santa Maria-quality Aquamarine — deeper, richer ocean-blue with exceptional purity — commands the highest prices. Brazilian origin remains the most widely trusted benchmark.</p>
        <p>Should it be certified? Always, for any stone of meaningful value. Certification confirms natural origin, treatment status, and protects against misrepresentation — particularly for stones represented as Santa Maria quality.</p>
        <p>Which metal pairs best? Silver and white gold complement the cool blue tones naturally and are the most commonly chosen. Yellow gold can work beautifully with warmer blue-green tones when the design supports it.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'pitambari' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--pitambari">
        <p>FAQs — Peetambari Sapphire</p>
        <p><strong>What is Peetambari Sapphire?</strong></p>
        <p>A natural bi-colour sapphire with yellow and blue in one crystal.</p>
        <p><strong>Are green-looking stones also Peetambari?</strong></p>
        <p>Yes. When colours blend finely, the stone may appear green overall.</p>
        <p><strong>Can it replace Yellow or Blue Sapphire?</strong></p>
        <p>No. It is not a substitute — it is chosen only for combined planetary balance.</p>
        <p><strong>What treatment should be avoided?</strong></p>
        <p>Diffusion treatment must be strictly avoided.</p>
        <p><strong>Should it be certified?</strong></p>
        <p>Always — certification is essential.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'purple-sapphire' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--purple_sapphire">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <p><strong>Is Purple Sapphire the same as Blue Sapphire or Ruby?</strong></p>
        <p>No. It is a natural variety of corundum with a colour balance between blue and red.</p>
        <p><strong>Is Khooni Neelam very powerful?</strong></p>
